But I think it's better to depend on Errata Tool and Pulp directly, instead of depending on them indirectly via SDEngine (the Product Security tool that publishes this repository-to-cpe.json mapping file). More details below:
 
Before https://redhat.atlassian.net/browse/SECDATA-841#icft=SECDATA-841
, the list of repositories was completely determined using Errata Tool and Pulp queries:
https://errata.engineering.redhat.com/documentation/developer-guide/api-http-api.html#get-apiv1cdn_repos
@classmethod
def get_all_repositories(cls):
    """Fetch all Pulp repos and their content sets"""
    data = {
        "criteria": {
            "fields": ["id", "notes"],
            "filters": {"notes.content_set": {"$ne": None}},
        }
    }
    response = cls.pulp_request(path="repositories/search/", data=data)
    return {
        repo["id"]: (repo["notes"]["content_set"], repo["notes"]["relative_url"])
        for repo in response.json()
    }
 
Now we're also querying the Git repo below for the UBI-related entries. See the three "output" keys in each file on the "ubi8" and "ubi9" branches, as well as the "ubi10" branch in the future (once it exists / is GA and not in beta anymore). For example:
https://gitlab.cee.redhat.com/ubi/data2/-/blob/ubi9/rhel-9-for-aarch64-appstream.yaml
These are the three sources of repository entries / data that SDEngine uses today, which Konflux might need to allow. I think a Pulp query (and maybe the UBI repo query too) should be enough, and the list of CDN repos in Errata Tool probably isn't needed by Konflux. I believe SDEngine only uses the Errata Tool data for some additional enrichment / linkage, which shouldn't be relevant here.

